**{{ message('meltdowns') }}.**

<!-- step: first -->
A meltdown shows in the daily numbers before it shows in the news. The detector scores every carrier's day, and every day at the {{ v('ch7.airports') }} busiest airports, against that carrier's or airport's own previous four weeks, on the share of flights cancelled and the mean arrival delay. Its alert threshold was chosen on {{ v('policy.fit_years') }} by cost, with a missed disruption costed at {{ v('ch7.cost_miss') }} false alarms, and landed at {{ v('ch7.threshold') }} robust standard deviations, {{ v('ch7.interior') }}. On the reporting years it flagged {{ v('ch7.test.detected') }} of the {{ v('ch7.test.events') }} disruptions in the known events table, at a cost of {{ v('ch7.test.false_per_thousand') }} false alarm episodes per thousand carrier and airport days.{% if has('ch7.study.WN.carrier') %} The chart opens on {{ v('ch7.study.WN.carrier') }} in {{ v('ch7.study.WN.onset') }}: its cancellation rate against every other carrier at its busiest airports ({{ v('ch7.study.WN.airports') }}), with the disruption shaded. The storm hit everyone; the gap is the carrier's own.{% endif %}

<!-- step: second -->
{% if has('ch7.study.DL.carrier') %}{{ v('ch7.study.DL.carrier') }} in {{ v('ch7.study.DL.onset') }} is the second panel: an outage of the software that runs its crew and aircraft scheduling, against the same kind of peer control. Over its window it cancelled {{ v('ch7.study.DL.excess_cancelled') }} more flights than its peers' rate implies, and its passengers sat through {{ v('ch7.study.DL.excess_delay_hours') }} of delay beyond theirs.{% else %}The second event study did not run on this data.{% endif %}{% if has('ch7.study.WN.carrier') %} {{ v('ch7.study.WN.carrier') }}'s excess over its window was {{ v('ch7.study.WN.excess_cancelled') }} flights and {{ v('ch7.study.WN.excess_delay_hours') }} of delay.{% endif %}

