{% if has('ch7.study.WN.carrier') and has('ch7.study.DL.carrier') %}Recovery is the number that separates them: {{ v('ch7.study.WN.carrier') }} took {% if raw('ch7.study.WN.recovery_days') is not none %}{{ v('ch7.study.WN.recovery_days') }} days{% else %}more than three weeks{% endif %} from onset to get back inside its own normal gap to its peers for three days running, and {{ v('ch7.study.DL.carrier') }} took {% if raw('ch7.study.DL.recovery_days') is not none %}{{ v('ch7.study.DL.recovery_days') }}{% else %}more than three weeks{% endif %}. {% endif %}Across every alert episode in the window, {% if raw('ch7.trait.p') is none %}too few carriers had enough alert episodes to test whether recovery speed is a trait of the carrier.{% elif raw('ch7.trait.p') < 0.05 %}carriers differ in how long their episodes last (Kruskal-Wallis H {{ v('ch7.trait.statistic') }}, permutation p {{ v('ch7.trait.p') }}, {{ v('ch7.trait.carriers') }} carriers): how fast an airline recovers is a property of the airline, not only of the storm.{% else %}carriers do not differ reliably in how long their episodes last (Kruskal-Wallis H {{ v('ch7.trait.statistic') }}, permutation p {{ v('ch7.trait.p') }}, {{ v('ch7.trait.carriers') }} carriers), so the two meltdowns above are not evidence of a carrier trait on their own.{% endif %}

